# Virtual or Not: The One Question

The midterm's 10-point OOD problem was a 3-line "what prints?" through a base pointer, in four variants. Every line comes down to one question: **is the method virtual in the base class?**

- **Not virtual:** the **pointer's (or reference's) declared type** picks the function, at compile time.

![without virtual the pointer type picks the function](courses/diagrams/be5110e3-6825-5df3-a950-93620046b7fd/plain.svg)

- **Virtual:** the **object's actual type** picks it, at run time. That's dynamic dispatch. If the object's class didn't override it, the nearest base version runs.

![with virtual the object type picks the function](courses/diagrams/be5110e3-6825-5df3-a950-93620046b7fd/virtual.svg)

Worked example:

```cpp
class Pet {
public:
    virtual string name() const { return "Pet"; }
    string noise() const { return "Hi"; }         // NOT virtual
    virtual ~Pet() {}
};
class Cat : public Pet {
public:
    string name() const { return "Cat"; }         // overrides
    string noise() const { return "Meow"; }       // only hides
};

Pet* p = new Cat();
cout << p->name() << " " << p->noise();           // Cat Hi
```

`name` is virtual in `Pet`, so the object (a Cat) decides. `noise` isn't, so the pointer type (`Pet*`) decides.

Three rules that decide the trick variants:

- What counts is `virtual` **in the base**. The derived method overrides it whether or not it repeats the word `virtual`.
- **Slicing:** copying a derived object into a plain base **object** keeps only the base part, so even virtual calls run the base version. A base **reference** or pointer doesn't slice.

![copying into a base object slices, a reference does not](courses/diagrams/b193fbb2-237e-583b-9fa2-4872fba4cf5f/slice.svg)

- Deleting a derived object through a base pointer needs a **virtual base destructor**. Without it the behavior is undefined.

> [!tip]
> On paper, mark every method V or not-V before you trace a single line.

