# Static Members and Where operator+ Lives

Two Quiz 1 T/F traps live here.

## Static members: one copy for the whole class

A **static data member** is not stored inside each object. There's exactly one, shared by every object, and a change made through any object is seen by all of them.

![two cafes with their own ids sharing one static counter](courses/diagrams/d5404960-7481-5543-88b0-94907e1aa0c6/shared.svg)

```cpp
class Cafe {
public:
    Cafe()  { num_open++; }
    ~Cafe() { num_open--; }
    static int getNumOpen() { return num_open; }   // callable as Cafe::getNumOpen()
private:
    static int num_open;
};
int Cafe::num_open = 0;   // defined once, outside the class
```

A **static member function** belongs to the class, so you can call it with no object. That means it has **no `this`**, and it can only use static members.

## operator+ doesn't have to be a member

`a + b` is just a call to a function named `operator+`. You can write it either way:

```cpp
// member: ONE parameter, the left side is *this
Money operator+(const Money& r) const;

// free function: TWO parameters; make it a friend if it reads private data
friend Money operator+(const Money& a, const Money& b);
```

So "an overloaded operator must be a member" is **false**. Only `=`, `[]`, `()` and `->` must be members, and `<<` with a stream on the left can't be one.

