# Recursion: Branches and Depth

Midterm Problem 13 asked for the Big O of recursive functions with a justification. Recursion has no loop to count, so count **calls**. Two questions:

- **b: how many recursive calls does each call make?**
- **d: how deep does it go?**

| b | Total calls | Big O |
|---|---|---|
| 1 | d + 1 | O(d) |
| 2 | 1 + 2 + 4 + … + 2ᵈ | O(2ᵈ) |
| 3 | 1 + 3 + 9 + … + 3ᵈ | O(3ᵈ) |

Then multiply by the work per call.

**Depth n (the argument drops by 1).** One call on n − 1, like `sumArray(arr, len - 1)`, is O(n) time, and also **O(n) stack space**, since every call waits until the one below returns. Two calls on n − 1:

```cpp
void g(int n) {
    if (n == 0) return;
    g(n - 1);
    g(n - 1);
}
```

![call tree of g(3) with 15 calls](courses/diagrams/f94b018d-ada8-5e24-9ad2-21b6ae0b3dc4/calls.svg)

b = 2, d = n: **O(2ⁿ)**. g(3) makes 1 + 2 + 4 + 8 = 15 calls. Three calls give O(3ⁿ).

**Depth log n (the argument halves).** Fast power calls itself **once** and saves the result:

```cpp
long power(long x, int n) {
    if (n == 0) return 1;
    long half = power(x, n / 2);
    if (n % 2 == 0) return half * half;
    else return x * half * half;
}
```

b = 1, d = log n: **O(log n)**. Write `power(x, n/2) * power(x, n/2)` instead and b becomes 2 with depth log n, which is 2^(log₂ n) = n leaves: **O(n)**. Same answer, slower.

![call tree of h(8) with 15 calls](courses/diagrams/f2c9ddad-0607-5b34-b950-27b9a2401d4f/halves.svg)

**Justify it like this:** "each call makes two recursive calls and n drops by one each time, so the depth is n and there are about 2ⁿ calls: O(2ⁿ)."

