# Same Code, Three Cases

Big O describes a growth rate. It doesn't say **which** input you mean, and two inputs of the same size can take very different times. So the same algorithm has three answers:

- **Worst case:** the hardest input. For linear search, the key is last or missing: O(n).
- **Average case:** the expected time over likely inputs. Quicksort is O(n log n) on average but O(n²) in the worst case.
- **Best case:** depends on the algorithm. It is **not always O(1)**.

Lab 3's `searchGrid` shows the best-case trap:

```cpp
for (int r = 0; r < rows; r++) {
    for (int c = 0; c < cols; c++) {
        if (grid[r][c] == target) {
            found = true;
            break;          // leaves only the INNER loop
        }
    }
}
```

Worst case, the target is missing: **O(rows · cols)**. Best case, `break` still only exits the inner loop, so the outer loop visits every row. With the target in column 0 of every row, each row costs one check: **O(rows)**.

The trap he sets: a T/F that quietly swaps the case. "Linear search is O(1)" is true only for the best case; unless a question says otherwise, Big O on his exams means the **worst case**.

