# Merge on Paper

**Merge** combines two sorted arrays into one. Keep an index into each: `i` into A, `j` into B, `k` into the output S. Compare the two fronts, copy the smaller into S, and advance only the index you copied from. When one array runs out, copy the rest of the other with **no** comparisons.

Merge `A = 1 4 9` with `B = 2 3 10`:

| Compare | Take | S so far |
|---|---|---|
| 1 vs 2 | 1 | `1` |
| 4 vs 2 | 2 | `1 2` |
| 4 vs 3 | 3 | `1 2 3` |
| 4 vs 10 | 4 | `1 2 3 4` |
| 9 vs 10 | 9 | `1 2 3 4 9` |
| A is empty | copy 10 | `1 2 3 4 9 10` |

Five comparisons. The skeleton he gives:

```cpp
while (i < lenA && j < lenB) {
    if (A[i] <= B[j]) {
        S[k] = A[i];   // copy A's front into S
        i++;           // move past it in A
    }
    else {
        S[k] = B[j];
        j++;
    } // end if
    k++;               // already there, after the if/else
} // end while
while (i < lenA) S[k++] = A[i++];
while (j < lenB) S[k++] = B[j++];
```

> [!warning]
> Write into **S**, never back into A or B. Each branch advances only its own index. Don't add another `k++` inside a branch when the skeleton has one after the if/else. And `<=` (take from A on a tie) is what makes merge sort stable.

**Merge sort** splits the array in half by **position**, sorts each half recursively, then merges. There are log₂ n levels of splitting, and each level merges all n keys once, so it's **O(n log n)** in the best, average and worst case. A nearly sorted array gets the same splits, since splitting ignores the values, so it is **not** faster. On arrays the merged results need a second array: **O(n)** extra space, so it is not in place.

Merge sort does its real work **after** the recursive calls, in merge. Quicksort does its real work **before** them, in partition.

